Complex UI/UX Systems in Figma
==============================

A complex UI/UX system in Figma refers to designing large-scale products that 
contain many screens, components, users, and interactions—like banking apps, 
dashboards, or enterprise software.

Instead of designing single pages, you build a full design system + connected flows.

1. What Makes a UI System “Complex”?
====================================
A UI/UX system becomes complex when it includes:
50+ screens or pages
Multiple user roles (admin, user, manager)
Large navigation systems
Dynamic data (dashboards, analytics)
Reusable components
Responsive layouts (mobile + desktop + tablet)

Example systems:
Banking apps
E-commerce platforms
Admin dashboards
Learning management systems (LMS)

2. Structure of a Complex Figma Project
=======================================
A professional Figma file is usually organized like this:
1. Design System
   - Colors
   - Typography
   - Buttons
   - Inputs

2. Components
   - Navbar
   - Sidebar
   - Cards
   - Tables

3. Pages / Screens
   - Login
   - Dashboard
   - Settings
   - Reports

4. Prototypes
   - User flows

3. Design Systems (Core of Complexity)
======================================
A design system is a collection of reusable UI elements.

Includes:
Color palette
Typography scale
Buttons
Icons
Spacing rules
Components

4. Components and Variants
==========================
Instead of designing everything repeatedly:

Use Components:
Button (Primary, Secondary, Disabled)
Input fields
Cards
Navigation bars

Use Variants:
Hover / Active / Disabled states
Dark mode / Light mode

Example:
One button → multiple styles

5. Auto Layout for Scaling
==========================
Auto Layout is essential for complex systems:
Responsive spacing
Dynamic resizing
Consistent alignment

Example:
Sidebar adjusts automatically when items are added

6. Multi-Screen Navigation (Prototyping)
========================================
Complex systems need flows:
Example:
Login → Dashboard → Reports → Settings → Logout

In Figma:
=========
Use Prototype links
Connect screens
Add transitions (slide, fade)

7. Dashboard Systems (Most Complex UI Type)
===========================================
Dashboards include:
Charts
Tables
Filters
KPIs (Key metrics)
Side navigation

Example systems:
Banking analytics
Sales dashboards
Admin control panels

8. Data-Heavy UI Design
=======================
Complex systems often show large data:
Tables with pagination
Search filters
Sorting
Real-time updates

Example:
User management system (1000+ users)

9. Responsive Design System
===========================
Design must work on:
Desktop (primary)
Tablet (medium layout)
Mobile (compact layout)

10. Collaboration in Large Projects
===================================
Figma supports teamwork:
Multiple designers working together
Developer handoff
Commenting system
Version history

11. File Organization Best Practices
====================================
For complex UI systems:
Use pages (Login, Dashboard, Settings)
Name components clearly
Use folders/sections
Maintain a style guide page
Avoid duplication

12. Example Real-World Complex System
=====================================
A banking app UI system includes:
Login + OTP
Account dashboard
Transactions
Loan management
Settings
Notifications
Summary

A complex UI/UX system in Figma is:
===================================
A structured, scalable design ecosystem made of reusable components, design systems,
multi-screen flows, and responsive layouts.

Simple Workflow
===============
Design System → Components → Screens → Prototype → Test → Iterate

